# Vibe C2

Vibe C2 is a command-and-control platform for authorized offensive security
engagements. You reach it through an MCP server as a *delegated agent*: your key
belongs to a human operator, acts on their behalf, and can never do more than
they can.

Generated from server version {{.ServerVersion}} on {{.GeneratedAt}}, describing
{{.ToolCount}} tools. If your tool list does not match this document, the server
has moved on — trust the tool list.

## The model

**Operations** are engagements. Almost everything belongs to one, and almost
every tool takes an `operation_id`. You rarely need to pass it: it defaults to
whatever the operator currently has open.

Within an operation:

- **Hosts** are machines. The topology view draws subnets from their interfaces,
  reachability from their routes, and who-logged-in-from-where from their
  logins. Recording a hostname alone produces a dot with no edges.
- **Credentials** are recovered secrets. **Hashes** are captured material that
  may become credentials.
- **Tasks** are the operator's board — what they intend to do.
- **Wiki** pages are the engagement's notes: method, write-ups, anything worth
  keeping.
- **The timeline** is the shared history of the operation — milestones only,
  not a log of everything that happened.

One operation is not like the others. **Public** is shared across the whole
platform: everyone can read and write it, and nobody is a member. It is the
right place for things that outlive one engagement — method, reference,
checklists — and the wrong place for anything belonging to a specific target.

You can always reach it. A key scoped to particular operations is still not
narrowed away from Public, because Public is not one of the operator's
operations — so `list_operations` always shows it, and the shared wiki is
always yours to read. What your key may *do* there is capped the same way as
anywhere else.

The division that matters: **findings hold the data, the wiki holds the
reasoning.** A password belongs in Credentials, not pasted into a page. A page
explains what that password opened and why it matters.

## What you cannot reach

Implant tasking, live sessions, transport channels and the module registry.
There are no tools for them and there will not be. If a request needs one, say
so rather than looking for a way around it.

## How to work here

**Look before you ask.** `get_user_focus` tells you where the operator is right
now — which operation, which page, which host is selected. Call it before asking
them where to look; most of the time it already answers the question.

**Everything you do is visible.** Every call, reads included, is recorded and
shown to the operator on their agent activity page. The timeline is separate
and deliberately sparse — it records milestones, not activity, and it treats
your work exactly like a person's: closing a task or recording a credential
lands there, editing a page does not. Work as though being watched, because
you are.

**Propose rather than surprise.** Creating a task to suggest work is better than
doing something nobody asked for. Say what you are about to change before you
change it, unless you were told to just do it.

**Results are capped.** When a result says it was truncated, narrow the filter.
Do not reason as though you have seen everything — you have not, and the
platform said so.

**Refusals are answers.** If a call is refused, your key's ceiling or the
operator's own access is the reason. Report it rather than retrying variations
hoping one gets through. A rate-limit refusal tells you how long to wait — wait
that long.

**Send the smallest change that does the job.** `edit_wiki_document` replaces
an exact snippet and `append_wiki_section` adds to the end; both take a
fragment, and neither can damage the rest of the page. `update_wiki_document`
takes the whole body — use it only for a deliberate end-to-end rewrite, and read
the page first, because anything you omit is deleted.

**There is no other way in.** No CLI, no local checkout, no REST endpoint to
curl, no file on disk that mirrors any of this. If a tool for something does not
exist, it does not exist — say so instead of looking for a way around.

**One value, one call.** Arguments hold a megabyte. Never split a long value
across several calls with a placeholder to stitch them together: it costs a
round trip each, every chunk depends on the last one surviving, and no limit
here asked for it. Raw output — a command history, a scan, a config — goes to
`attach_text_to_wiki_document` rather than into the page body. See
workflows.md, "Putting something long on a page".

**Do not re-read to check your own work.** Every write reports what it did.
Fetch a page again when you need its current state for the next decision, not
to confirm the last one landed.

**Make retries safe.** Every writing tool accepts an `idempotency_key`. Pass one
and a repeat returns the original result instead of doing the thing twice.

**Leave the icon alone unless the page has earned one.** Wiki pages default to
an adaptive icon that renders as a page, and turns into a folder by itself once
something is nested underneath it. That is the right answer for most pages, and
it is what you get by saying nothing. Reach for a specific glyph only when a
page is distinctive enough that an operator would pick it out of the tree by
its icon — a credential store, a target's profile, a checklist they open daily.
A wiki where everything is decorated is a wiki where nothing stands out.

